434	|	 	|	0	|	0	|		|	The virus family, Ascoviridae, to which this virus belongs is a recently \nproposed virus family. (1983).  The reference in which this virus fi\nfamily was first proposed is PNAS 80:7664-7668 (Federici).\nThe ascoviruses, including the isolate from which I have submitted sequence,\nSpodoptera ascovirus, are a family of dsDNA viruses.  The genomes of\nthe ascoviruses range in size from ca. 140kb to 180 kb and are linear.\nThe dsDNA genomes of ascovirusesare packaged in large, enveloped virions.\nThese virions exhibit complex symmetry, have a highly reticulated surface,\nand range in shape from reniform to bacilliform.	|	43680 	|
435	|	 	|	0	|	0	|		|	The Spodoptera ascovirus was first described in The Florida Entomologist\n69:524-531 by Hamm et al.\n\nThe viruses of this family are not well characterized which likely accounts\nfor their absence from the International virus taxonomy publications.\nTheascoviruses, including the isolate from which I have submitted sequence,\nSpodoptera ascovirus, are a family of dsDNA viruses.  The genomes of\nthe ascoviruses range in size from ca. 140kb to 180 kb and are linear.\nThe dsDNA genomes of ascoviruses arepackaged in large, enveloped virions.\nThese virions exhibit complex symmetry, have a highly reticulated surface,\nand range in shape from reniform to bacilliform.\n\nAscoviruses have been isolated from several genera of lepidopteran noctuids,\nincludingSpodoptera, Heliothis, Scrotogramma, Trichoplusia, and Autographa.	|	43681 	|
