12483	|	Psammophis mossambicus:	|	0	|	0	|		|	The name Psammophis mossambicus (described from Mozambique Island by\nPeters in 1882, as Psammophis sibilans var mossambica) was first\nformally applied by Branch (1998). This has been largely followed in\nthe subsequent literature, and Broadley (2002) selected a lectotype in\nBerlin (ZMB 2468A) and confirmed that the name applies to the large\nsouthern and south-east African Olive Whip Snake.\n\nRefs:\nBranch, W. R. 1998. Bill Branch's Field Guide to the Snakes and Other\nReptiles of Southern Africa. Revised Edition. 326pp. Struik, Cape\nTown.\n\nBroadley, D. G. 2002. A review of the species of Psammophis Boie\nfound south of Latitude 12deg. S (Serpentes: Psammophiinae). African\nJournal of Herpetology 51 (2):83-119.	|	234064 	|
