25001	|	Yokochi T. & Yago, M. (per. comm.)	|	0	|	0	|		|	The genus Cynitia Snellen, 1895, is a replacing name for Felderia Semper, 1888, since the latter is a junior homonym of Felderia Walsingham, 1887. The type species has been Felderia phlegethon Semper, 1888, which was subsequently designated by de Niceville (1893).\n\nSince the original description, this genus has been used by researchers such as Moore (1897) and Fruhstorfer (1913), although Bingham (1905) and Corbet (1956) recognized the genus a junior synonym of Euthalia Hubner, 1819.\n\nEliot (1978) treated Cynitia as a junior synonym of Tanaecia Butler, 1869 because of similarities in their body structure, as well as in the venation, pattern and habits. However, he did not show concrete characteristics and behaviors for this taxonomic treatment. Additionally, he indicated that Cynitia is available if a separate name is needed for the cocytus group. This classification has been used by many following butterfly taxonomists.\n\nRecently, Tsukada (1991) resurrected Cynitina containing the following 15 species: cocytus, lepidea, sipora, iapis, cocytina, flora, godartii, vuiana, fontanus, peloris, pegasis, phlegethon, semperi, telchinia and whiteheadi. According to this study, these species could not be assigned to either Euthalia or Tanaecia based on the external morphology, biology and male genitalia. A conclusion was reached that the genus Cynitia should be divided from Tanaecia to accommodate above species and be placed between Euthalia and Tanaecia. In particular, Cynitia has distinct androconia in the cells 6 and 7 of the hindwing upperside (Euthalia and Tanaecia usually do not have them); veins 6 and 7 of the forewing in Cynitia and Euthalia are fused at base, while those in Tanaecia are separated from each other. This classification is followed by recent studies, e. g., Nishiyama (1999).	|	658579 	|
