38621	|	gencode:1:The Standard Code:010	|	0	|	0	|		|	By default all transl_table in GenBank flatfiles are equal to id 1, and this is {b}not{b} shown.  When transl_table is not equal to id 1, it is shown as a qualifier on the CDS feature.\n\n{@GC1@}\n\n{head}Initiation Codon:{head}\nAUG\n\n{head}Alternative Initiation Codons:{head}\nIn rare cases, translation in eukaryotes can be initiated from codons other than AUG. A well documented case (including direct protein sequencing) is the GUG start of a ribosomal P protein of the fungus\n{link}{url}/Taxonomy/Browser/wwwtax.cgi?name=Candida+albicans{url}Candida albicans{link} ({link}{pubmed}12961752{pubmed}Abramczyk et al.{link}) and the GUG initiation in mammalian NAT1 ({link}{pubmed}15718103{pubmed}Takahashi et al. 2005{link}). \nOther examples can be found in the following references: {link}{pubmed}2538469{pubmed}Peabody 1989{link}; {link}{pubmed}2538817{pubmed}Prats et al. 1989{link}; {link}{pubmed}3277717{pubmed}Hann et al. 1992{link}; {link}{pubmed}2123874{pubmed}Sugihara et al. 1990{link}. The standard code currently allows initiation from UUG and CUG in addition to AUG.	|	1 	|
38622	|	Thermolongibacillus (LPSN link)	|	0	|	0	|	http://www.bacterio.net/thermolongibacillus.html	|		|	1500514 	|
38623	|	Youngiibacter (LPSN link)	|	0	|	0	|	http://www.bacterio.net/youngiibacter.html	|		|	1408818 	|
38624	|	gencode:2:The Vertebrate Mitochondrial Code:020	|	0	|	0	|		|	{@GC2@}\n\n{head}Differences from the Standard Code:{head}\n{pre}\n        Code 2          Standard\n\n AGA    Ter  *          Arg  R\n AGG    Ter  *          Arg  R\n AUA    Met  M          Ile  I\n UGA    Trp  W          Ter  *\n\n{pre}\n\n{head}Alternative Initiation Codons:{head}\nBos: AUA\nHomo: AUA, AUU\nMus: AUA, AUU, AUC\nCoturnix, Gallus: also GUG ({link}{pubmed}1706782{pubmed}Desjardins and Morais, 1991{link})\n\n{head}Systematic Range:{head}\nVertebrata\n\n{head}Comments:{head}\n\nAGA and AGG were thought to have become mitochondrial stop codons early in vertebrate evolution ({link}{pubmed}2506356{pubmed}Osawa, Ohama, Jukes & Watanabe 1989{link}). However, at least in humans it has now been shown that AGA and AGG sequences are not recognized as termination codons. A -1 mitoribosome frameshift occurs at the AGA and AGG codons predicted to terminate the CO1 and ND6 ORFs, and consequently both ORFs terminate in the standard UAG codon ({link}{pubmed}20075246{pubmed}Temperley et al. 2010{link}).\nMitochondrial genes in some vertebrate (including humans) have incomplete stop codons ending in U or UA, which become complete termination codons (UAA) upon subsequent polyadenylation ({link}{pubmed}17205108{pubmed}Hou et al. 2006{link}; {link}{pubmed}17541835{pubmed}Oh et al. 2007{link}; {link}{pubmed}19757186{pubmed}Ki et al. 2010{link}; {link}{pubmed}20211597{pubmed}Temperley R J et al 2010{link}).	|	1 	|
38625	|	gencode:3:The Yeast Mitochondrial Code:030	|	0	|	0	|		|	{@GC3@}\n\n{head}Differences from the Standard Code:{head}\n{pre}\n        Code 3          Standard\n\n AUA    Met  M          Ile  I\n CUU    Thr  T          Leu  L\n CUC    Thr  T          Leu  L\n CUA    Thr  T          Leu  L\n CUG    Thr  T          Leu  L\n UGA    Trp  W          Ter  *\n\n{pre}\n{head}Systematic Range:{head}\n\n{i}Saccharomyces cerevisiae{i}, {i}Candida glabrata{i}, {i}Hansenula saturnus{i}, and {i}Kluyveromyces thermotolerans{i} ({link}{pubmed}8083884{pubmed}Clark-Walker and Weiller, 1994{link})\n\n{head}Comments:{head}\nGUG (GTG) is used as a start codon for a few proteins in some {i}Saccharomyces{i} species  ({link}{pubmed}28992063{pubmed}Sulo et al. 2017{link}). The remaining CGN codons are rare in {i}Saccharomyces cerevisiae{i} and absent in {i}Candida glabrata{i} (= {i}Torulopsis glabrata{i}).\n\n The AUA codon is common in the gene var1 coding for the single mitochondrial ribosomal protein, but rare in genes encoding the enzymes.\n\nThe coding assignments of the AUA (Met or Ile) and CUU (possibly Leu, not Thr) are uncertain in {i}Hansenula saturnus{i}.\n\nThe coding assignment of Thr to CUN is uncertain in {i}Kluyveromyces thermotolerans{i} ({link}{pubmed}8083884{pubmed}Clark-Walker and Weiller, 1994{link}).	|	1 	|
38627	|	gencode:4:The Mold, Protozoan, and Coelenterate Mitochondrial Code and the Mycoplasma/Spiroplasma Code:040	|	0	|	0	|		|	{@GC4@}\n\n{head}\nDifferences from the Standard Code:\n{head}\n{pre}\n        Code 4         Standard\n\n UGA    Trp  W          Ter  *\n\n{pre}\n\n{head}Alternative Initiation Codons:{head}\n\n{b}Trypanosoma{b}: UUA, UUG, CUG   \n{b}Leishmania{b}: AUU, AUA \n{b}Tertrahymena{b}: AUU, AUA, AUG\n{b}Paramecium{b}: AUU, AUA, AUG, AUC, GUG, GUA(?) \n({link}{pubmed}2137906{pubmed}Pritchard et al., 1990{link})\n\n{head}Systematic Range:{head}\n\n{b}Bacteria{b}: The code is used in {i}Entomoplasmatales{i} and {i}Mycoplasmatales{i} ({link}{pubmed}7691196{pubmed}Bove et al. 1989{link}). The situation in the {i}Acholeplasmatales{i} is unclear. Based on a study of ribosomal protein genes, it had been concluded that UGA does not code for tryptophan in plant-pathogenic mycoplasma-like organisms (MLO) and the {i}Acholeplasmataceae{i} ({link}{pubmed}1556079{pubmed}Lim and Sears, 1992{link}) and there seems to be only a single tRNA-CCA for tryptophan in {i}Acholeplasma laidlawii{i} ({link}{pubmed}2762159{pubmed}Tanaka et al. 1989{link}). In contrast, in a study of codon usage in {i}Phytoplasmas{i}, it was found that 30 out of 78 ORFs analyzed translated better with code 4 (UGA for tryptophan) than with code 11 while the remainder showed no differences between the two codes ({link}{pubmed}14594823{pubmed}Melamed et al. 2003{link}). In addition, the coding reassignment of UGA Stop --> Trp can be found in an alpha-proteobacterial symbiont of cicadas: {i}Candidatus Hodgkinia cicadicola{i} ({link}{pubmed}19609354{pubmed}McCutcheon et al. 2009{link}).\n\n{b}Fungi{b}: {i}Emericella nidulans{i}, {i}Neurospora crassa{i}, {i}Podospora anserina{i}, {i}Acremonium{i} ({link}{pubmed}3327473{pubmed}Fox, 1987{link}), {i}Candida parapsilosis{i} ({link}{pubmed}1826652{pubmed}Guelin et al., 1991{link}), {i}Trichophyton rubrum{i} ({link}{pubmed}1326416{pubmed}de Bievre and Dujon, 1992{link}), {i}Dekkera/Brettanomyces, Eeniella{i} ({link}{pubmed}8387113{pubmed}Hoeben et al., 1993{link}), and probably {i}Ascobolus immersus{i}, {i}Aspergillus amstelodami{i}, {i}Claviceps purpurea{i}, and {i}Cochliobolus heterostrophus{i}.\n\n{b}Other Eukaryotes{b}: {i}Gigartinales{i} among the red algae ({link}{pubmed}8190631{pubmed}Boyen et al. 1994{link}), and the protozoa {i}Trypanosoma brucei{i}, {i}Leishmania tarentolae{i}, {i}Paramecium tetraurelia{i}, {i}Tetrahymena pyriformis{i} and probably {i}Plasmodium gallinaceum{i} ({link}{pubmed}2779560{pubmed}Aldritt et al., 1989{link}).\n\n{b}Metazoa{b}: {i}Coelenterata{i} (Ctenophora and Cnidaria)\n\n{head}Comments:{head}\nThis code is also used for the kinetoplast DNA (maxicircles, minicircles).  Kinetoplasts are modified mitochondria (or their parts).	|	1 	|
38628	|	gencode:5:The Invertebrate Mitochondrial Code:050	|	0	|	0	|		|	{@GC5@}\n\n{head}Comment:{head}\nThe codon AGG is absent in Drosophila.\n\n{head}Differences from the Standard Code:{head}\n{pre}\n\n        Code 5          Standard\n\n AGA    Ser  S          Arg  R\n AGG    Ser  S          Arg  R\n AUA    Met  M          Ile  I\n UGA    Trp  W          Ter  *\n\n{pre}\n\n{head}Alternative Initiation Codons:{head}\nAUA, AUU\nAUC: Apis ({link}{pubmed}8417993{pubmed}Crozier and Crozier, 1993{link})\nGUG: Polyplacophora ({link}{pubmed}7828825{pubmed}Boore and Brown, 1994{link} GenBank Accession Number: \n{link}{url}http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/nuccore/557273?report=GenBank{url}U09810{link})\nUUG: Ascaris, Caenorhabditis\n\n{head}Systematic Range:{head}\n{b}Nematoda{b}: Ascaris, Caenorhabditis;\n{b}Mollusca{b}: Bivalvia ({link}{pubmed}1386586{pubmed}Hoffmann et al., 1992{link}); Polyplacophora ({link}{pubmed}7828825{pubmed}Boore and Brown, 1994{link})\n{b}Arthropoda/Crustacea{b}: Artemia ({link}{pubmed}3135541{pubmed}Batuecas et al., 1988{link});\n{b}Arthropoda/Insecta{b}: Drosophila [Locusta migratoria (migratory locust), Apis mellifera (honeybee)]\n\n{head}Comments:{head}\nSeveral arthropods translate the codon AGG as lysine instead of serine (as in the invertebrate mitochondrial genetic code) or arginine (as in the standard genetic code) ({link}{pubmed}16620150{pubmed}Abascal et al., 2006{link}).\n\nGUG may possibly function as an initiator in Drosophila ({link}{pubmed}3001325{pubmed}Clary and Wolstenholme, 1985{link}; {link}{pubmed}3399396{pubmed}Gadaleta et al., 1988{link}). AUU is not used as an initiator in Mytilus ({link}{pubmed}1386586{pubmed}Hoffmann et al., 1992{link}).\n\n\"An exceptional mechanism must operate for initiation of translation of the cytochrome oxidase subunit I mRNA in both D. melanogaster ({link}{pubmed}6408489{pubmed}de Bruijn, 1983{link}) and D. yakuba ({link}{pubmed}6314262{pubmed}Clary and Wolstenholme 1983{link}), since its only plausible initiation codon, AUA, is out of frame with the rest of the gene. Initiation appears to require the \"reading\" of of an AUAA quadruplet, which would be equivalent to initiation at AUA followed immediately by a specific ribosomal frameshift.  Another possible mechanism ... is that the mRNA is \"edited\" to bring the AUA initiation into frame.\" ({link}{pubmed}3327473{pubmed}Fox, 1987{link})	|	1 	|
38629	|	gencode:6:The Ciliate, Dasycladacean and Hexamita Nuclear Code:060	|	0	|	0	|		|	{@GC6@}\n\n{head}Differences from the Standard Code:{head}\n{pre}\n          Code 6       Standard\n\n UAA      Gln  Q        Ter  *\n UAG      Gln  Q        Ter  *\n\n{pre}\n\n{head}Systematic Range:{head}\n\n{b}Ciliata{b}: Oxytricha and Stylonychia ({link}{pubmed}7753617{pubmed}Hoffman et al. 1995{link}), Paramecium, Tetrahymena, Oxytrichidae and probably Glaucoma chattoni.\n{b}Dasycladaceae{b}: Acetabularia ({link}{pubmed}2573818{pubmed}Schneider et al., 1989{link}) and \n{b}Batophora{b} ({link}{pubmed}1934113{pubmed}Schneider and de Groot, 1991{link}).\n\n{b}Diplomonadida{b}: \nScope: Hexamita inflata, Diplomonadida ATCC50330, and ATCC50380. \n\nRef.: {link}{pubmed}8641293{pubmed}Keeling, P.J. and Doolittle, W.F. 1996.{link}.  A non-canonical genetic code in an early diverging eukaryotic lineage. The EMBO Journal 15, 2285-2290.\n\n{head}Comment:{head}\nThe ciliate macronuclear code has not been determined completely. The codon UAA is known to code for Gln only in the Oxytrichidae.	|	1 	|
38630	|	gencode:9:The Echinoderm and Flatworm Mitochondrial Code:090	|	0	|	0	|		|	{@GC9@}\n\n{head}Differences from the Standard Code:{head}\n{pre}\n\n          Code 9        Standard\n\n AAA      Asn  N        Lys K\n AGA      Ser  S        Arg R\n AGG      Ser  S        Arg R\n UGA      Trp  W        Ter *\n\n{pre}\n\n{head}Systematic Range:{head}\n\n{b}Asterozoa{b} (starfishes) ({link}{pubmed}3678836{pubmed}Himeno et al., 1987{link})\n{b}Echinozoa{b} (sea urchins) ({link}{pubmed}3172215{pubmed}Jacobs et al., 1988{link}; {link}{pubmed}2544576{pubmed}Cantatore et al., 1989{link})\n{b}Rhabditophora{b} among the Platyhelminthes ({link}{pubmed}11027335{pubmed}Telford et al. 2000{link})	|	1 	|
38631	|	gencode:10:The Euplotid Nuclear Code:100	|	0	|	0	|		|	{@GC10@}\n\n{head}Differences from the Standard Code:{head}\n{pre}\n          Code 10     Standard\n\n UGA      Cys  C        Ter  *\n\n{pre}\n\n{head}Systematic Range:{head}\n\n{b}Ciliata{b}: Euplotidae ({link}{pubmed}7753617{pubmed}Hoffman et al. 1995{link}).	|	1 	|
38632	|	gencode:11:The Bacterial, Archaeal and Plant Plastid Code:110	|	0	|	0	|		|	{@GC11@}\n\n{head} Systematic Range and Comments:{head}\n\nTable 11 is used for Bacteria, Archaea, prokaryotic viruses and chloroplast proteins. As in the standard code, initiation is most efficient at AUG. In addition, GUG and UUG starts are documented in Archaea and Bacteria ({link}{pubmed}6343825{pubmed}Kozak 1983{link}, {link}{pubmed}3521591{pubmed}Fotheringham et al. 1986{link}, {link}{pubmed}7592355{pubmed}Golderer et al. 1995{link}, {link}{pubmed}7730278{pubmed}Nolling et al. 1995{link}, {link}{pubmed}8946162{pubmed}Sazuka & Ohara 1996{link}, {link}{pubmed}9821671{pubmed}Genser et al. 1998{link}, {link}{pubmed}14633098{pubmed}Wang et al. 2003{link}). In E. coli, UUG is estimated to serve as initiator for about 3% of the bacterium's proteins ({link}{pubmed}9278503{pubmed}Blattner et al. 1997{link}). CUG is known to function as an initiator for one plasmid-encoded protein (RepA) in Escherichia coli ({link}{pubmed}1447126{pubmed}Spiers and Bergquist, 1992{link}). In addition to the NUG initiations, in rare cases Bacteria can initiate translation from an AUU codon as e.g. in the case of poly(A) polymerase PcnB and the InfC gene that codes for translation initiation factor IF3 ({link}{pubmed}1660923{pubmed}Polard et al. 1991{link}, {link}{pubmed}8405963{pubmed}Liveris et al. 1993{link}, {link}{pubmed}8946162{pubmed}Sazuka & Ohara 1996{link}, {link}{pubmed}12068810{pubmed}Binns & Masters 2002{link}). The internal assignments are the same as in the standard code though UGA codes at low efficiency for Trp in Bacillus subtilis and, presumably, in Escherichia coli ({link}{pubmed}8488562{pubmed}Hatfiled and Diamond, 1993{link}).	|	1 	|
38633	|	gencode:12:The Alternative Yeast Nuclear Code:120	|	0	|	0	|		|	{@GC12@}\n\n{head}Differences from the Standard Code:{head}\n{pre}\n           Code 12      Standard\n\n CUG       Ser          Leu\n       \n{pre}\n\n{head}Alternative Initiation Codons:{head}\n\nCAG may be used in Candida albicans ({link}{pubmed}8440250{pubmed}Santos et al., 1993{link}).\n\n{head}Systematic Range and Comments:{head}\n\nIn code 12 the CUG codon has been reassigned from Leu to Ser ({link}{pubmed}21819941{pubmed}Santos et al., 2011{link}, {link}{pubmed}23619021{pubmed}Mateus et al., 2013{link}, {link}{pubmed}29760453{pubmed}Krassowski et al., 2018{link}). This code is used by the fungal CUG-Ser1 and CUG-Ser2 clades that represent separate monophyletic lineages of the Saccharomycotina ({link}{pubmed}33607033{pubmed}Li et al., 2021{link}). In the NCBI taxonomy, CUG-Ser1 currently comprises the three families Cephaloascaceae, Debaryomycetaceae and Metschnikowiaceae and the genus Babjeviella. The CUG-Ser1 clade includes important human pathogens like Candida albicans and the multidrug-resistant Candida auris. The CUG-Ser2 clade represents a parallel reassignment of CUG and includes species in the families Ascoideaceae and Saccharomycopsidaceae. Most other true yeasts (Saccharomycotina) use the standard code however.	|	1 	|
38634	|	gencode:13:The Ascidian Mitochondrial Code:130	|	0	|	0	|		|	{@GC13@}\n\n{head}Differences from the Standard Code:{head}\n{pre}\n          Code 13     Standard\n\n AGA      Gly  G        Arg  R\n AGG      Gly  G        Arg  R\n AUA      Met  M        Ile  I\n UGA      Trp  W        Ter  *\n\n{pre}\n\n{head}Systematic range and Comments:{head}\n\nThere is evidence from a phylogenetically diverse sample of tunicates (Urochordata) that AGA and AGG code for glycine. In other organisms, AGA/AGG code for either arginine or serine and in vertebrate mitochondria they code a STOP. Evidence for glycine translation of AGA/AGG has been found in Pyura stolonifera ({link}{pubmed}8393993{pubmed}Durrheim et al. 1993{link}), Halocynthia roretzi ({link}{pubmed}10352185{pubmed}Kondow et al. 1999{link},{link}{pubmed}8381878{pubmed}Yokobori et al., 1993{link}, {link}{pubmed}10581290{pubmed}Yokobori et al. 1999{link}) and Ciona savignyi ({link}{pubmed}14738316{pubmed}Yokobori et al. 2003{link}).\n\nIn addition, the Halocynthia roretzi mitochondrial genome encodes an additional tRNA gene with the anticodon U*CU that is thought to enable the use of AGA or AGG codons for glycine and the gene has been shown to be transcribed in vivo ({link}{pubmed}10352185{pubmed}Kondow et al. 1999{link}, {link}{pubmed}10581290{pubmed}Yokobori et al. 1999{link}).\n\n{head}Alternative initiation codons:{head}\nATA, GTG and TTG ({link}{pubmed}10581290{pubmed}Yokobori et al. 1999{link}). ATT is the start codon for the CytB gene in {i}Halocynthia roretzi{i} ({link}{url}http://www.pubmedcentral.nih.gov/articlerender.fcgi?tool=pubmed&pubmedid=12915488{url}Gissi and Pesole, 2003{link}).	|	1 	|
38635	|	Amouric A et al. (2014)	|	0	|	24030688	|		|	Amouric, A., Liebgott, P.-P., Joseph, M., Brochier-Armanet, C., and Lorquin, J. \"Halomonas olivaria sp. nov., a moderately halophilic bacterium isolated from olive-processing effluents.\" Int. J. Syst. Evol. Microbiol. (2014) 64:46-54.	|	390919 	|
38636	|	Liu JJ et al. (2014)	|	0	|	24014623	|		|	Liu, J.-J., Zhang, X.-Q., Chi, F.-T., Pan, J., Sun, C., and Wu, M. \"Gemmobacter megaterium sp. nov., isolated from coastal planktonic seaweeds.\" Int. J. Syst. Evol. Microbiol. (2014) 64:66-71.	|	1086013 	|
38638	|	Wu YH et al. (2014)	|	0	|	24030689	|		|	Wu, Y.-H., Xu, L., Meng, F.-X., Zhang, D.-S., Wang, C.-S., Oren, A., and Xu, X.-W. \"Altererythrobacter atlanticus sp. nov., isolated from deep-sea sediment.\" Int. J. Syst. Evol. Microbiol. (2014) 64:116-121.	|	1267766 	|
38639	|	gencode:14:The Alternative Flatworm Mitochondrial Code:140	|	0	|	0	|		|	{@GC14@}\n\n{head}Differences from the Standard Code:{head}\n{pre}\n          Code 14      Standard\n\n AAA      Asn  N       Lys  K\n AGA      Ser  S       Arg  R\n AGG      Ser  S       Arg  R\n UAA      Tyr  Y       Ter  *\n UGA      Trp  W       Ter  *\n\n{pre}\n\n{head}Systematic Range:{head}\n\n{b}Platyhelminthes (flatworms) and Nematoda (roundworms){b}\n\n{head}Comments:{head}\n\nCode 14 differs from code 9 only by translating UAA to Tyr rather than STOP. A recent study ({link}{pubmed}11027335{pubmed}Telford et al. 2000{link}) has found no evidence that the codon UAA codes for Tyr in the flatworms but other opinions exist. There are very few GenBank records that are translated with code 14 but a test translation shows that retranslating these records with code 9 can cause premature terminations. More recently, UAA has been found to code for tyrosine in the nematodes Radopholus similis and Radopholus arabocoffeae ({link}{pubmed}19778425{pubmed}Jacob et al. 2009{link}).	|	1 	|
38640	|	gencode:15:Blepharisma Nuclear Code:150	|	0	|	0	|		|	{@GC15@}\n\n{head}Differences from the Standard Code:{head}\n{pre}\n          Code 15       Standard\n\nTAG       Gln  Q        STOP\n{pre}\n\n{head}Systematic Range:{head}\n\n{b}Crassvirales{b}: {link}{pubmed}36175428{pubmed}Experimental validation that human microbiome phages use alternative genetic coding. Peters et al., Nature Communications 2022{link}	|	1 	|
38641	|	gencode:16:Chlorophycean Mitochondrial Code:160	|	0	|	0	|		|	{@GC16@}\n\n{head}Differences from the Standard Code:{head}\n{pre}\n          Code 16       Standard\n\nTAG       Leu  L        STOP\n\n{pre}\n\n{head}Systematic Range:{head}\n\n{b}Chlorophyceae{b} ({link}{pubmed}8662206{pubmed}Hayashi-Ishimaru et al. 1996{link}. UAG is a sense codon in several chlorophycean mitochondria) and the chytridiomycete fungus {b}Spizellomyces punctatus{b} ({link}{pubmed}9016605{pubmed}Laforest et al. 1997{link}. Mitochondrial tRNAs in the lower fungus Spizellomyces punctatus: tRNA editing and UAG 'stop' codons recognized as leucine).	|	1 	|
38642	|	gencode:21:Trematode Mitochondrial Code:210	|	0	|	0	|		|	{@GC21@}\n\n{head}Differences from the Standard Code:{head}\n{pre}\n          Code 21       Standard\n\nTGA       Trp  W        STOP\nATA       Met  M        Ile\nAGA       Ser  S        Arg\nAGG       Ser  S        Arg\nAAA       Asn  N        Lys \n{pre}\n\n{head}Systematic Range:{head}\n\n{b}Trematoda{b}: {link}{pubmed}2111847{pubmed}Ohama, T, S. Osawa, K. Watanabe, T.H. Jukes, 1990.  J. Molec Evol. 30{link}\n{link}{pubmed}2545889{pubmed}Garey, J.R. and D.R. Wolstenholme, 1989.  J. Molec. Evol. 28: 374-387 329-332.{link}	|	1 	|
38643	|	gencode:22:Scenedesmus obliquus Mitochondrial Code:220	|	0	|	0	|		|	{@GC22@}\n\n{head}Differences from the Standard Code:{head}\n{pre}\n          Code 22       Standard\n\nTCA       STOP *        Ser\nTAG       Leu  L        STOP\n\n{pre}\n\n{head}Systematic Range:{head}\n\n{b}Scenedesmus obliquus{b}: {link}{pubmed}10854413{pubmed}Nedelcu A, Lee RW, Lemieux C, Gray MW and Burger G. \"The complete mitochondrial DNA sequence of Scenedesmus obliquus reflects an intermediate stage in the evolution of the green algal mitochondrial genome.\"{link} Genome Res. 2000 Jun;10(6):819-31.	|	1 	|
38644	|	gencode:23:Thraustochytrium Mitochondrial Code:230	|	0	|	0	|		|	{@GC23@}\n\nThis code has been created for the mitochondrial genome of the labyrinthulid Thraustochytrium aureum sequenced by The Organelle Genome Megasequencing Program ({link}{url}http://megasun.bch.umontreal.ca/ogmpproj.html{url}OGMP{link}).\n\nIt is the similar to the bacterial code ({link}{url}#11{url}transl_table 11{link}) but it contains an additional stop codon (TTA) and also has a different set of start codons.	|	1 	|
38645	|	gencode:24:Rhabdopleuridae Mitochondrial Code:240	|	0	|	0	|		|	{@GC24@}\n\n{head}Differences from the Standard Code:{head}\n{pre}\n          Code 24        Standard\n\nAGA       Ser S          Arg  R\nAGG       Lys K          Arg  R\nUGA       Trp W          STOP *\n\n{pre}\n\nCode 24 has been created for the mitochondrial genome of Rhabdopleura compacta (Pterobranchia). The Pterobranchia are one of the two groups in the Hemichordata which together with the Echinodermata and Chordata form the three major lineages of deuterostomes. AUA translates to isoleucine in Rhabdopleura as it does in the Echinodermata and Enteropneusta while AUA encodes methionine in the Chordata. The assignment of AGG to Lys is not found elsewhere in deuterostome mitochondria but it occurs in some taxa of Arthropoda ({link}{pubmed}21599892{pubmed}Perseke et al. 2011{link}). Code 24 shares with many other mitochondrial codes the reassignment of the UGA STOP to Trp, and AGG and AGA to an amino acid other than Arg. The initiation codons in Rhabdopleura compacta are ATG and GTG ({link}{pubmed}21599892{pubmed}Perseke et al. 2011{link}).	|	1 	|
38646	|	gencode:25:Candidate Division SR1 and Gracilibacteria Code:250	|	0	|	0	|		|	{@GC25@}\n\n{head}Differences from the Standard Code:{head}\n{pre}\n          Code 25        Standard\n\nUGA       Gly            STOP\n{pre}\n\n\n{head}Initiation Codons:{head}\nAUG, GUG, UUG\n\n{head}Systematic Range:{head}\n\n{b}Candidate Division SR1{b}, {b}Gracilibacteria{b}\n\n{head}Comments:{head}\n\nCode 25 is used in two groups of (so far) uncultivated Bacteria found in marine and fresh-water environment and in the intestines and oral cavities of mammals among others. The difference to the standard and the bacterial code is that UGA represents an additional glycine codon and does not code for termination ({link}{pubmed}23509275{pubmed}Campbell et al. 2013{link}).	|	1 	|
